## 0. Epistemic legend

Every claim below carries one of these tags. They are not interchangeable, and the report does not let one collapse into another anywhere below.

| Tag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **[ADOPTED ARCHITECTURE]** | A decided, binding record in the corpus (e.g., a decision record). |
| **[DRAFT ARCHITECTURE]** | Stated in a corpus document marked non-binding / drafted-for-review (e.g., the concept of operations). |
| **[THEOREM]** | Proved ‚Äî either machine-checked in the corpus (Lean, "zero-axiom" or otherwise) or an externally cited proved result. |
| **[CONJECTURE]** | Explicitly open in the corpus, or open in the cited external scholarship. |
| **[OPERATIONAL INTERPRETATION]** | A working reading the corpus itself proposes without proof. |
| **[HISTORICAL CLAIM]** | A claim about Daoist or Chinese intellectual history, sourced to the supplied scholarship. |
| **[METAPHOR]** | Figurative language. No formal or historical content is claimed to transfer. |
| **[NEW CONSTRUCTION, THIS REPORT]** | Built here, for this report, not present in the corpus under this name. |
| **[EVIDENTIARY GAP]** | The premise requires an observation the corpus does not contain. |

