## 5. What classical ACO/stigmergy convergence requires, and what fails or is unproved here

The corpus contains a real, cited convergence theorem and two real, in-house failure results. Neither transfers to the MetroPlat-pulse question without new work.

**Requirements of the classical result** (Source 9, St√ºtzle‚ÄìHoos MAX-MIN Ant System, cited as **[THEOREM]** ‚Äî external, "status: proved"):

- H1: lower-bounded pheromone, $\tau_{\min} > 0$, on every edge at every time;
- H2: bounded deposition per agent per iteration;
- H3: positive evaporation, $\rho \in (0,1)$;
- H4: an elitist archive (best-so-far retained and re-deposited every iteration).

Under H1‚ÄìH4, $\Pr[s^{\text{best}}_t = s^*] \to 1$. Remove any one hypothesis and the proof breaks (stated explicitly in Source 9).

Broader classical stigmergy (Source 8, tracing Grass√© 1959 through Bonabeau‚ÄìDorigo‚ÄìTheraulaz 1999) additionally presupposes:

- H5: a persistent shared medium that decouples agents in space and time;
- H6: the scalability premise ‚Äî many, cheap, roughly homogeneous agents;
- H7: local-rule coordination / local determinism.

Classical multi-agent convergence more generally (Source 6, consensus/formation/flocking) requires connectivity or rootedness of the interaction graph and, per Source 7's sharper formalization, **symmetric handedness**: $\mathrm{recv}_i = \mathrm{send}_j$ for all pairs and symmetric $\mathrm{actuate}$ across participants (H8).

**What fails or is unproved for LLM-agent / heterogeneous settlement systems:**

- **H6 and H7 fail**, per the corpus's citation of Doan et al. (arXiv 2506.14496, June 2025): LLM agents violate the scalability premise by orders of magnitude, and natural-language coordination violates local-rule discipline. This is reported in-corpus as an **[EXTERNAL, CITED RESULT]**; this report has not independently verified it.
- **H8 fails**, per an in-house result ‚Äî but that result is marked, in its own frontmatter, `status: sketch`, `proof-target: subsequent-session` (Source 7). It shows heterogeneous, receive-mostly, partial-observability, precondition-bounded participants violate uniform reciprocal exchange. This is a **[CONJECTURE / PROOF SKETCH]**, not a completed theorem, and it was stated about drone collectives, not about inter-settlement climate coupling; extending it to $E_i \leftrightarrow M$ coupling is a further, unproved analogical step this report is *not* taking as established.
- Source 7 also proposes that a substrate-side "structural stigmergy" layer converges regardless of agent-layer handedness, via "closure-2-monad lax-idempotence." This report has only the theorem's *name*, cited but not included in the supplied corpus text ‚Äî its content cannot be checked here. It is therefore doubly unverifiable: unproved at its own stated status, and uninspectable from the supplied packet.
- **H1‚ÄìH4 (the actual ACO hypotheses) have no defined analogue anywhere in the corpus** for an inter-settlement pulse channel. Nothing proposes a bounded, evaporating trace-field or an elitist archive for the $E_i$‚Äì$M$ relation. The question "does ACO convergence apply to the MetroPlat pulse" is therefore not "formalized and failing" but **not yet formalizable** from what exists.

**Conclusion for this section:** no probability-one convergence guarantee, from ACO, PSO, consensus, or classical stigmergy, may be inherited by any pulsar-typed reading of the MetroPlat channel. Any convergence claim about settlements coordinating coherently under an exogenous pulse must be built fresh from the corpus's own settlement/closure apparatus (¬ß2 above), not borrowed.

