LEADER DEMAND
Test the newly proposed interpretation:
- MetroPlat's external cadence may be an "ANT-Colony Pulsar": a force or signal outside a settlement's climate that produces a pulse through the system without gaining write authority inside sovereign settlements.
- the distal bypass may be read as the taiji of the ANT colony;
- xiantian qi and wuji may offer historically disciplined comparative lenses.
This vocabulary is absent from the existing corpus and is NOT inherited doctrine.

Required report:
- Give a formal candidate model with settlements E_i, local climates c_i(t), exogenous pulse p(t), observation/coupling maps, and explicit denial of external write authority.
- Separate at least these interpretations of pulsar: cadence, force, demand, price signal, scheduler, energy/resource input. Do not identify them silently.
- Give a matrix: Leader term; corpus carrier; historical Daoist source/scholarship; proposed correspondence; epistemic status; incompatibility/falsifier.
- Explain the distal bypass precisely before testing the taiji comparison.
- State which hypotheses classical ACO/stigmergy convergence requires and which fail or remain unproved for LLM-agent systems.
- Treat Ming-era history carefully. Do not flatten wuji, taiji, or xiantian; do not label Song-era Zhou Dunyi as Ming Daoism.
- Distinguish analogy, design discipline, historical genealogy, and formal correspondence.
- State nonclaims: qi is not joules or information; no proof distal bypass "is" taiji; no legal extraterritoriality; no inherited swarm convergence theorem.
- Provide falsifiers and engineering consequences for the MetroPlat pulse/arrival/capability circuit.
- End with a bounded recommendation: what vocabulary is usable now, what must remain proposed, and what further sources/formal work are required.
- Aim for postgraduate technical fidelity, not mystical decoration and not hostile debunking.

Externally checked scholarship supplied by the commissioning office:
1. Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Religious Daoism" (Summer 2021 archive), https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2021/entries/daoism-religion/ ‚Äî summarizes Dao as wuji and taiji and describes precosmic shen/qi/jing stages. This is a modern secondary overview, not a Ming primary source.
2. Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Chinese Metaphysics", https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-metaphysics/ ‚Äî places taiji in Yijing cosmology and Song Neo-Confucian development. This is essential evidence against casually calling Zhou Dunyi's formulation Ming Daoism.
3. Chinese Text Project entry for Xingming guizhi, https://ctext.org/datawiki.pl?if=en&res=217252 ‚Äî bibliographic carrier for a 1615 Ming neidan text, syncretic and illustrated.
4. Chinese Religious Text Authority entry C71, https://crta.info/wiki/%E6%80%A7%E5%91%BD%E5%9C%AD%E6%97%A8_-_C71 ‚Äî bibliographic authority for Xingming guizhi.
5. Brill, "Visualizing Perfection: The Images of the Alchemical Body in the Xingming guizhi", https://brill.com/display/book/9789004522756/BP000022.pdf ‚Äî specialist chapter treating the 1615 illustrations and visualization as transformative practice oriented toward fundamental cosmic order.
