## 12a. The prime census (2026-07-11, prime-focused session)

Operator-directed sweep of the library's prime-number content. Every
claim re-verified against the Lean this session (fresh `lean_run_code`
audits, not the cached environment).

- **The fundamental theorem of arithmetic is proved and choice-free.**
  `Infomaterial.Factorization.prime_factorization_exists`,
  `prime_factorization_unique`, bundled as
  `fundamental_theorem_of_arithmetic`
  (`Infomaterial/Calculus/Order/Factorization.lean`). Audited:
  propext and Quot.sound only ‚Äî no Classical.choice; likewise
  `NumberTheory.Prime.euclid` and `primes_top_interval_prod_le`. The
  same module proves the image factorization of functions
  (`factorization_system`: every map = surjection then injection) ‚Äî
  factoring a map through its image and factoring an integer through
  primes as one module's subject.

- **The Bertrand/Chebyshev engine exists without its capstone**
  (`Infomaterial/Calculus/Order/NumberTheoryCombinatorics.lean`):
  `Prime.dvd_choose_central` (a prime in (m+1, 2m+1] divides
  C(2m+1,m), via the `Prime.dvd_factorial` /
  `Prime.not_dvd_factorial` sandwich),
  `primes_top_interval_prod_dvd_choose_central`, and
  `primes_top_interval_prod_le` (product of distinct top-interval
  primes ‚â§ 2^(2m)) ‚Äî the induction step of the Chebyshev primorial
  bound ‚àè_{p‚â§x} p ‚â§ 4^x. Neither that bound nor Bertrand's postulate
  is stated anywhere in the tree.

- **Euclid's infinitude of primes is absent.** No declaration states
  the primes are unbounded. All ingredients are green: n!+1 ‚â• 2 has a
  prime factor (`prime_factorization_exists`); a prime q ‚â§ n divides
  n! (`Prime.dvd_factorial`); a common divisor of n! and n!+1 divides
  1 (needs only a small Dvd-subtraction lemma not yet in the tree);
  `Prime.two_le` closes it. Inherits at most the propext/Quot.sound
  debt of its inputs.

- **The Collatz residue core is elementary number theory about 2 and
  3** (`Closure/Collatz/ResidueGap.lean`): B√©zout witnesses over ‚Ñ§,
  modular inverses, residue characterization of the two-generator
  semigroup, `three_pow_mod_two`, `coprime_odd_two_pow`,
  `parentM_eq_pow` (modulus = 2^(len+1)). The one sorry
  (`parentBarrierGap_holds`, `SemigroupGap.lean`) is equivalent to a
  residue-overshoot statement for 3-power affine combinations against
  a 2-power modulus.

- **ArithmeticZetaStar is 2-vs-3 arithmetic**
  (`AperiodicOrder/Tiling/ArithmeticZetaStar.lean`): the affine trace
  has denominator exactly 2^depth (`zetaStarTrace_B_eq_two_pow`) and
  numerator exactly 3^oddCount
  (`zetaStarTrace_A_eq_three_pow_oddCount`), with
  `zetaStar_shortcut_twoAdicValuation` measuring the 2-adic collapse
  per odd step. Diffraction dichotomy: pure point ‚ü∫ Pisot inflation,
  singular continuous ‚ü∫ Salem (`zetaStar_phase_singularContinuous_iff_salem`).
  Reading (not theorem): Pisot/Salem classes are defined by conjugate
  location against the unit circle ‚Äî the RH thread's Artin‚ÄìMazur red
  node plugs in here.

- Readings, not theorems: the `Quark.lean` Œ∂ with Œ∂¬≤ = Œ∂ ‚àí 1 is the
  Eisenstein-integer unit; in ‚Ñ§[Œ∂] the prime 3 ramifies, 2 is inert,
  p ‚â° 1 mod 3 splits ‚Äî none of this is stated in the tree. And
  561 = 3¬∑11¬∑17 is the least Carmichael number (naming observation
  only; no claim).

**The prime program, ranked (operator-endorsed this session):**

1. **Euclid's infinitude** ‚Äî near-free; extend `Factorization.lean`
   (the owning module), plus its blueprint node.
2. **Bertrand's postulate** ‚Äî the two hard divisibility bricks are
   green; missing are the primorial bound assembled by induction, the
   central-binomial lower bound 4^n ‚â§ (2n+1)¬∑C(2n,n), the
   prime-power bound p^k ‚à• C(2n,n) ‚Üí p^k ‚â§ 2n, and the Erd≈ës
   assembly. State the primorial bound as its own theorem first.
3. **The Pad√© log‚ÇÇ3 measure** toward `parentBarrierGap` (¬ß5; Collatz
   memory) ‚Äî the constructive irrationality-measure step; linear
   forms in logarithms have never been formalized in any proof
   assistant (¬ß10).
4. **The Artin‚ÄìMazur zeta on the odometer** ‚Äî stage n is a single
   2^n-cycle (`vershikIter_reaches`), so periodic-point counts of
   odometer iterates have closed forms; weld the counting zeta to the
