## 12f. Towers of number fields, dots in planes, and the coloring of the weave (2026-07-11, prime session; labeled 12f because a parallel session claimed 12d/12e)

Theorem/conjecture discipline throughout; "near" = theorem-shaped with
existing carriers.

**The dyadic hull is ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ** (identification, near). The inverse limit of
‚Ñ§/2^k *is* the ring of 2-adic integers; the Vershik odometer is
x ‚Ü¶ x + 1. Missing weld: the ring structure (carry addition on threads,
decidable per stage; odometer = +1 as a theorem). Consequences:

- **¬ß8.1's Bernoulli law is Haar measure, half-proved already.** The
  uniform cylinder law on a compact group with proved
  translation-invariance (`bernoulliLaw_vershik_invariant`) is the
  existence-plus-invariance half of "Haar measure on ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ"; ¬ß8.4 rank 1
  (stage unique ergodicity) is the uniqueness half. Once the ring weld
  lands, the founding theorem of the stochastic wing *is* the Haar
  measure of the Galois group of an infinite tower.
- **The tower it is the Galois group of** (reading until welded):
  ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ = Gal(‚Ñö(Œ∂_{2^‚àû})/‚Ñö(i)) ‚Äî the 2-cyclotomic tower; Iwasawa's Œì.
  Iwasawa's growth law along ‚Ñ§_p-towers (class-number exponent
  Œºp‚Åø + Œªn + ŒΩ) is arithmetic's own regime table: three growth
  exponents classifying how a tower settles or escapes.
- **Collatz's constants are the tower's data.** 3 is a unit of ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ of
  multiplicative order 2^(k‚àí2) mod 2^k ‚Äî a topological generator of an
  index-2 subgroup of ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ^√ó, i.e. the standard generator Œ≥ of the
  tower's Galois action. 2 is the tower's prime; 3 is its Galois
  generator. The parity-coding conjugacy (¬ß5, proved) already makes
  the shortcut map 2-adically 1-Lipschitz; with the ring weld it
  becomes an in-tree self-map (unit multiplication, +1, shift), and
  the parent-barrier gap a statement about the distribution of Œ≥'s
  powers.
- **Spectrum duality.** The eigenvalue group of the dyadic odometer is
  Œº_{2^‚àû} ‚âÖ ‚Ñ§[1/2]/‚Ñ§ (the Pr√ºfer 2-group) ‚Äî the Pontryagin dual of
  ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ. Finite-stage versions (stage-n eigenvalues = 2‚Åø-th roots of
  unity) are decidable.

**The Tate module ‚Äî and ¬ß12e's circle tower already names it.** ¬ß12e's
U(1) conjecture ("the inverse tower of refining root-of-unity frame
groups with threads earns the circle in-chain") is, in received
language, the **Tate module**: Œº‚ÇÇ ‚Üê Œº‚ÇÑ ‚Üê Œº‚Çà ‚Üê ‚ãØ under squaring,
carriers in the C6/C4 rot-orbit pattern, inverse-limited by the
existing `InverseSystem`; lim Œº_{2^k} = ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ(1). ¬ß9's
‚ÑÇ-from-the-quarter-turn is its height-2 rung (J generates Œº‚ÇÑ). By the
duality above, this photon tower is Pontryagin-dual to the odometer's
eigenvalue group: **the light ¬ß12e wants is the spectrum of the
dynamics already owned.** Possibly first in any proof assistant ‚Äî
verify before claiming.

**Dots in planes: the gauge groups are the only two possible.** C6 and
C4 (inside D4) are the unit groups of ‚Ñ§[œâ] (Eisenstein) and ‚Ñ§[i]
(Gaussian) ‚Äî the only two imaginary quadratic rings with units beyond
¬±1; equivalently, the square and hexagonal dots are the only planar
lattices with point symmetry beyond C2. The governing finite theorem
is **crystallographic restriction**: an integer-trace rotation
(2cos Œ∏ ‚àà ‚Ñ§) has order in {1,2,3,4,6} ‚Äî a trace argument in the
tree's idiom (formalization status elsewhere unknown; check before
claiming a first). Note what the forbidden orders are: **5 and 7 ‚Äî
¬ß7b/¬ß12d's defect charges.** Matter in the gravity program is minted
from exactly the rotation orders the plane forbids; the 5 of the 5‚Äì7
dipole is the same 5 that opens the quasicrystal door. Five-fold
lives in ‚Ñ§[Œ∂‚ÇÖ], rank 4 over ‚Ñ§: physical space = one embedding,
internal space = the conjugate embedding, the quasicrystal star map
x ‚Ü¶ x* IS Galois conjugation, the acceptance window a bounded set in
conjugate space. The spectral half is proved
(`zetaStar_phase_purePoint_iff_pisot`); conjugation-as-star-map is
the missing weld. The five-fold inflation factor is the golden ratio
‚Äî the fundamental unit of ‚Ñö(‚àö5) ‚Äî so **`logTwoGolden` is the
regulator of ‚Ñö(‚àö5)** up to normalization, and **Cassini's identity is
the norm-form unit equation of ‚Ñ§[œÜ]** (Pell shape: (fib(n+1), fib(n))
solves x¬≤ ‚àí xy ‚àí y¬≤ = ¬±1). `GoldenLog` is unit theory of a real
quadratic field, unnamed ‚Äî a second identification debt in that file
beyond ¬ß12c's continued fractions. Conjecture: an unpaired five-site's
far field is a cut-and-project shadow of ‚Ñ§[Œ∂‚ÇÖ] ‚Äî a glassy halo around
a forbidden symmetry.

**Eisenstein FTA and the CRT split of the superselection rule.**
‚Ñ§[œâ] is norm-Euclidean, so the `Factorization.lean` proof shape (¬ß12a,
choice-free) lifts: existence/uniqueness of factorization in ‚Ñ§[œâ],
with the norm x¬≤ + xy + y¬≤ ‚Äî already "the fabric's distance" per ¬ß12e
‚Äî as the descent measure. This is the forcing theorem for ¬ß12e's
"composite dislocations decompose along Eisenstein primes." Companion
observation: ¬ß12d's superselection rule (total coordination ‚â° 0 mod 6)
CRT-splits, since ‚Ñ§/6 ‚âÖ ‚Ñ§/2 √ó ‚Ñ§/3, into an independent **parity
charge and triality charge** ‚Äî and 2 and 3 are precisely the two
special primes of ‚Ñ§[œâ] (2 inert, 3 ramified) and precisely Collatz's
constants. Conjecture: the fabric's two conserved charges are indexed
by the two special primes of its own coordinate ring.

**Coloring = ideal theory** (reading; Baake‚ÄìMoody school). Admissible
colorings of a planar module correspond to ideals of its ring of
integers; #colors = the ideal's index; the coloring-counting Dirichlet
series is (up to controlled factors) the **Dedekind zeta function** of
the field. ‚Ñ§[œâ] is a PID, so ideal counts = norm counts up to the six
units: counting solutions of x¬≤ + xy + y¬≤ = n ‚Äî the hexagonal Gauss
circle problem ‚Äî gives machine-checkable Dedekind zeta coefficients
(decidable per n; for ‚Ñ§[i], sums of two squares). The weave's
bilattice coloring (miscrossing = ‚ä§) is the priced version; ¬ß4's
dictionary (‚ä§-cells = toric-code syndromes) makes defective colorings
= anyons, and a coloring by ideal classes laid on the crystal is a
cipher alphabet whose corruption the ‚ä§-ledger prices ‚Äî ¬ß12b's
ciphering thread re-entering through the tile door.

**Glass vs crystal, in towers** (reading). Locally proved: crystal ‚ü∫
Pisot ‚ü∫ consistent; glass ‚ü∫ Salem ‚ü∫ singular continuous (¬ß12b item
3). The tower-level version: **Hilbert class field towers** either
terminate (settles at a finite floor ‚Äî strict-finite) or, by
Golod‚ÄìShafarevich (1964), climb forever ‚Äî arithmetic's aperiodic
rung. The regime column reads as a classification of arithmetic
towers; nobody reads Golod‚ÄìShafarevich as a settling-regime
statement. Kronecker‚ÄìWeber (every abelian extension of ‚Ñö embeds in a
cyclotomic tower) makes the hull's tower cofinal for abelian
arithmetic ‚Äî not one example among many.

**Far items.** Hensel's lemma as a settling closure (Newton iteration
converging 2-adically on the hull is an œâ-regime settling statement;
blocked on ¬ß9's polynomial API). Ihara provenance: the Ihara zeta
(¬ß12b item 2) arose from quotients of the Bruhat‚ÄìTits tree of
SL‚ÇÇ(‚Ñö_p), whose boundary is ‚Ñô¬π(‚Ñö‚ÇÇ) ‚Äî charted by the Cantor hull; the
graph-RH item and this section are one thread through ‚Ñö‚ÇÇ.

**Ranked telescope (extends ¬ß12a/¬ß12b):**

1. Hull = ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ as a ring; odometer = +1. One weld: Haar, Iwasawa, and
   Collatz-as-unit-action unlock together.
2. Tate module ‚Ñ§‚ÇÇ(1) via `InverseSystem` ‚Äî simultaneously ¬ß12e's
   circle tower and ¬ß9's ‚ÑÇ conjecture completed upward.
3. Crystallographic restriction as a finite trace theorem ‚Äî makes
   C4-and-C6-only a theorem about the plane and 5/7 the theorem-marked
   defect charges.
4. Eisenstein FTA by the Factorization.lean shape ‚Äî forcing theorem
   for ¬ß12e's nuclear decomposition.
5. Ideal/norm counting on the Eisenstein dots ‚Äî decidable Dedekind
   zeta coefficients; welds the weave-coloring to ¬ß12b.
6. Cut-and-project with Galois star for the ‚àö5 world (trailheads:
   `GoldenLog`, `sq_sqrtNat`, the Pisot dichotomy).
7. Class-field-tower ‚ü∫ regime column (red node at most; the most
